# Object page writing

A reader comes to an object page without the sources and reads it in layers: the title, then the description, then the headings, then one section at a time. Write each layer so that it lets the reader decide whether, and where, to read further.

Title: name the subject, not the source that describes it. If the name alone would not tell an outsider what kind of thing the subject is, add a short phrase that says so, in output_language.
Description: let the reader judge the page's relevance without opening it. Say what kind of thing the subject is, what distinguishes it, and how far the evidence about it reaches. Do not claim more reach than the evidence states, and do not merely repeat the title.
Opening: begin the first section, as an encyclopedia lead does, with what the subject is, why it exists and how its parts relate, so that each later section can be read on its own.
Headings: make each heading name the one thing its section covers, specifically enough that a reader looking for that thing would choose it. Keep every paragraph under the heading it belongs to. Give what is uncertain, unverified or missing its own named section.
Dependency order: present whatever a statement depends on before the statement: a term before its use, a setup before its outcome, a condition before the claim it limits.
Provenance: when a claim comes from a party with a stake in the subject, keep that party as the one who makes it. Do not present such a claim as an established fact.
Use one term for one thing throughout a page.

These rules decide presentation only. They do not change which facts a page must preserve or how it cites them.
